The highest rewards so far all use a negative weight on cart position (to pull the cart back toward the center) and a strong positive weight on pole angle (to move the cart under the leaning pole). However, they still drift because the position and velocity gains are relatively weak, leading to early termination around 60–80 steps. By increasing the magnitude of the position weight (params[0] = -5.0) we should correct cart displacement more aggressively. Strengthening the velocity damping (params[2] = -1.0) will reduce drift caused by cart motion, while a slightly higher angular‑velocity gain (params[3] = 2.5) gives a faster response to pole rotation. Keeping the angle gain high (params[1] = 6.0) and bias zero maintains the standard pole‑catching behavior. This combination is expected to balance pole stabilization and cart recentering, potentially extending episode length toward the target horizon.
